1
00:00:00,790 --> 00:00:03,150
Hello everyone, this
is Dr. Carl Abraham

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:05,890
from the Jonesboro
campus presenting this

3
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:10,650
lecture on emerging
blood-borne pathogens.

4
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:16,740
The principles today are to
review the epidemiologics,

5
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:19,750
virulence factors, and
clinical presentations

6
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:23,490
of emerging blood-borne
pathogens, and to review

7
00:00:23,490 --> 00:00:26,510
the diagnostic techniques
and treatments for them.

8
00:00:26,730 --> 00:00:29,350
We're going to compare and
contrast these diseases

9
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:32,410
caused by different species
of single genuses, for

10
00:00:32,410 --> 00:00:36,590
example, Bartonella, Plasmodium,
and Leishmania species.

11
00:00:37,290 --> 00:00:41,800
I've included a QR code that
goes to the ScholarRxBrick

12
00:00:42,010 --> 00:00:45,690
for protozoa and
ectoparasites on this slide.

13
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:49,850
Let's review a few key
concepts that will be

14
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:53,430
important during this
lecture. A zoonosis is a

15
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:56,790
human disease derived
from an animal reservoir.

16
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,910
A vector is a living
organism, typically

17
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:03,250
an arthropod, that transmits an

18
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:05,810
infection from one
host to another.

19
00:01:06,290 --> 00:01:10,070
A life cycle is the
phases of growth and

20
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:13,330
transmission that
result in maturity and

21
00:01:13,330 --> 00:01:17,010
reproduction of an
organism, often requiring

22
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:19,830
intermediate and
definitive hosts. hosts.

23
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:24,390
And infestation is the
term we use for the

24
00:01:24,390 --> 00:01:28,290
presence of parasites
or ectoparasites on the

25
00:01:28,290 --> 00:01:31,810
body's surface. We don't
use the term infection.

26
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:44,690
Started with our first
organism. There are many

27
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:47,130
Bartonella species that
cause human disease,

28
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:49,790
but we are going to look
at just three of them.

29
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:53,810
Bartonella henselae,
Quintana, and Bacilliformis.

30
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:56,770
These are pleomorphic
gram-negative rods.

31
00:01:56,930 --> 00:01:59,650
Pleomorphic means the
shape of the bacteria can

32
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:02,610
vary, as opposed to the
uniform shape of, for example,

33
00:02:02,610 --> 00:02:05,870
E. coli or Klebsiella
when seen on Gram stain.

34
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:10,270
Bartonella are catalase
and oxidase negative. They

35
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:13,970
infect CD34 cells that
are the progenitors of

36
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:18,250
endothelial cells, as well
as erythrocytes, resulting

37
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:21,930
in dissemination throughout
the endovasculature,

38
00:02:21,930 --> 00:02:26,290
spread to the lymphatics,
lymph nodes, and rarely

39
00:02:26,290 --> 00:02:30,070
organs such as the heart,
liver, spleen, and bone.

40
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:34,210
Bartonella grow very slowly
in culture, taking up to

41
00:02:34,210 --> 00:02:37,950
two to six weeks, and they
are not well visualized

42
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:41,630
on gram stain. They are
well visualized using the

43
00:02:41,630 --> 00:02:45,230
Worthing Starry Silver
Stain, an example of which is

44
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:48,270
shown on the bottom
right, the darkly staining

45
00:02:48,270 --> 00:02:52,190
Bartonella organisms seen in
the center of the picture.

46
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:57,550
Hensley is the etiologic
agent of cat scratch disease.

47
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:02,230
It also causes bacillary
angiomatosis and Bartonella

48
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:06,250
peleiosis. It is geographically
distributed worldwide.

49
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:10,450
There are about 25,000
cases annually in the U.S.

50
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:13,950
It is transmitted by
cat fleas by a cat

51
00:03:13,950 --> 00:03:17,970
scratch or bite. After
a 3-5 day incubation

52
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:21,550
period, a small red
papule, vesicle, or

53
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:23,770
nodule appears at the
site of inoculation.

54
00:03:24,510 --> 00:03:26,870
That is followed by regional

55
00:03:26,870 --> 00:03:30,090
lymphadenopathy,
fever, and malaise.

56
00:03:30,090 --> 00:03:33,970
This is usually a self
-limited illness in those

57
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:37,630
who are immune-competent.
Very rarely, it can

58
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:40,640
go on to cause culture
-negative endocarditis,

59
00:03:40,810 --> 00:03:44,310
fever of unknown origin,
and neuroretinitis.

60
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:48,450
Bartonella pelliosis is
a condition of the liver

61
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:51,990
or spleen in which
endothelial proliferation

62
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:55,730
results in enlarged
capillaries forming saccules

63
00:03:55,730 --> 00:03:59,490
within these organs. It
is sometimes noted only

64
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:03,110
incidentally on imaging,
but it can help to suggest

65
00:04:03,110 --> 00:04:05,590
the diagnosis in the
right clinical setting.

66
00:04:05,890 --> 00:04:08,990
Many patients with
self-limited disease do

67
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:11,450
not even report to a
healthcare provider to get

68
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:14,450
a diagnosis and treatment,
but the diagnosis

69
00:04:14,450 --> 00:04:18,790
can be made by PCR,
biopsy of skin lesions,

70
00:04:18,910 --> 00:04:21,510
lymph nodes, or
affected organs that

71
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:25,230
show stellate
necrotizing granulomas.

72
00:04:25,490 --> 00:04:27,990
The diagnosis can
also be made by

73
00:04:27,990 --> 00:04:30,170
serum antibodies
or blood cultures.

74
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:33,150
Of note, blood
cultures need to be

75
00:04:33,150 --> 00:04:36,210
held for many weeks
due to slow growth.

76
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:38,510
Treatment, if needed, is with

77
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:40,990
azithromycin or doxycycline.

78
00:04:41,350 --> 00:04:44,210
and treatment is
always indicated in

79
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:46,530
immune-suppressed
persons who can have

80
00:04:46,530 --> 00:04:49,230
extremely severe
or fatal disease.

81
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:54,330
Bartonella quintana causes
trench fever and bacillary

82
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:58,670
angiomatosis. It is so
named because soldiers in

83
00:04:58,670 --> 00:05:02,790
World War II fighting in
trenches were widely infected.

84
00:05:03,310 --> 00:05:07,630
It occurs worldwide, mostly
in settings of poverty,

85
00:05:07,630 --> 00:05:10,910
overcrowding, and in those
experiencing homelessness.

86
00:05:11,350 --> 00:05:14,450
It is transmitted by
the feces of body lice.

87
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:18,030
Symptoms are unique for
Quentin fever, a fever

88
00:05:18,030 --> 00:05:21,770
that recurs every five
days, although this exact

89
00:05:21,770 --> 00:05:24,690
pattern is not seen in
all patients, that is

90
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:29,130
accompanied by rash,
headache, back and leg pain.

91
00:05:29,170 --> 00:05:31,590
These symptoms may abate and

92
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:34,170
then relapse after
days or weeks.

93
00:05:34,790 --> 00:05:37,070
Bartonella Quintana
can also rarely

94
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:39,570
cause culture
-negative endocarditis.

95
00:05:40,110 --> 00:05:42,610
Diagnosis is by PCR, blood or

96
00:05:42,610 --> 00:05:44,550
tissue culture, or serology.

97
00:05:44,570 --> 00:05:47,970
The prognosis is usually
quite good after prolonged

98
00:05:47,970 --> 00:05:50,730
treatment with doxycycline
and gentamicin.

99
00:05:51,070 --> 00:05:55,190
Bacillary angiomatosis,
caused by either Bartonella

100
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:58,530
henselae or quintana,
usually requires treatment

101
00:05:58,530 --> 00:06:01,910
with long-term erythromycin
or doxycycline.

102
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:06,470
The acute phase of
Bartonella bacilliformis

103
00:06:06,470 --> 00:06:11,190
infection is called Orroia
or carrion fever, while

104
00:06:11,190 --> 00:06:14,710
the chronic phase is
called Verruga perroiana.

105
00:06:15,230 --> 00:06:19,670
Infections occur at the
high-altitude Andean valleys

106
00:06:19,670 --> 00:06:23,270
of Peru and in parts of
Ecuador and Colombia, where

107
00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:26,730
outbreaks can occur that
involve thousands of cases.

108
00:06:26,770 --> 00:06:31,050
It is transmitted by the bite
of the sandfly. Incubation

109
00:06:31,050 --> 00:06:34,810
is 3 to 12 weeks, after
which infected persons have

110
00:06:34,810 --> 00:06:39,590
fever, weakness, myalgia,
and arthralgias. This acute

111
00:06:39,590 --> 00:06:43,490
phase is commonly associated
with a severe hemolytic

112
00:06:43,490 --> 00:06:47,990
anemia that may involve
up to 80% of erythrocytes.

113
00:06:48,130 --> 00:06:51,730
Those with chronic
infection experience Verruga

114
00:06:51,730 --> 00:06:56,930
Peruana, a rash that is
also termed Peruvian warts.

115
00:06:57,170 --> 00:07:00,290
Diagnosis is by blood
culture or peripheral

116
00:07:00,290 --> 00:07:04,170
smear. Those who are treated
have a good prognosis,

117
00:07:04,170 --> 00:07:07,630
but untreated, it is
associated with a mortality

118
00:07:07,630 --> 00:07:11,890
rate of 90%. Acute
infection is treated with

119
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:15,190
serprofloxacin and
ceftriaxone. Chronic

120
00:07:15,190 --> 00:07:19,510
infection is treated with
rifampin or streptomycin.

121
00:07:21,390 --> 00:07:25,730
Skin and soft tissue signs
are an important part of

122
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:29,710
the diagnosis of Bartonella
infection. A papule or

123
00:07:29,710 --> 00:07:32,930
nodule at the site of cat
scratch or bite associated with

124
00:07:32,930 --> 00:07:36,730
subsequent regional
lymphadenopathy, as seen on the

125
00:07:36,730 --> 00:07:40,530
top right, is characteristic
of cat scratch disease.

126
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:45,030
Bacillary angiomatosis
caused by Bartonella henselae

127
00:07:45,030 --> 00:07:48,750
or Bartonella quintana is a
condition that occurs only

128
00:07:48,750 --> 00:07:52,030
in the severely immune
suppressed, for example, in those

129
00:07:52,030 --> 00:07:55,610
living with advanced AIDS.
It is caused by capillary

130
00:07:55,610 --> 00:07:58,670
proliferation that results
in cutaneous papules and

131
00:07:58,670 --> 00:08:02,350
nodules. This appearance
is very similar to that of

132
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:06,170
Kaposi's sarcoma, which
is also seen in severely

133
00:08:06,170 --> 00:08:11,230
immunosuppressed persons.
Therefore, the diagnosis is made by

134
00:08:11,230 --> 00:08:14,370
biopsy of the lesions,
which shows neutrophilic

135
00:08:14,370 --> 00:08:20,010
infiltrates, lump endothelial
cells, termed epithelioid cells,

136
00:08:20,010 --> 00:08:23,890
and basophilic aggregates
of Bartonella organisms.

137
00:08:24,010 --> 00:08:27,610
PCR can also be performed
on tissue, and the

138
00:08:27,610 --> 00:08:30,350
organism can be isolated
from blood cultures.

139
00:08:30,630 --> 00:08:34,330
An example of bacillary
angiomatosis is

140
00:08:34,330 --> 00:08:36,610
shown in the middle
picture on the right.

141
00:08:37,090 --> 00:08:41,570
Verruga peruana is the
chronic eruptive phase of

142
00:08:41,570 --> 00:08:45,470
Carrion's disease caused by
Bartonella bacilliformis.

143
00:08:45,470 --> 00:08:49,830
It is due to localized
angioplastic hyperplasia,

144
00:08:49,830 --> 00:08:53,550
or capillary ***********,
that results in blood

145
00:08:53,550 --> 00:08:57,650
-filled vascular nodules
called verrugas. An

146
00:08:57,650 --> 00:09:00,630
example is shown in the
picture on the bottom right.

147
00:09:02,090 --> 00:09:06,270
The next bacteria we'll
talk about is the Borrelia.

148
00:09:06,380 --> 00:09:08,930
They are a class
of Spirochete that

149
00:09:08,930 --> 00:09:11,870
includes also Treponema
and Leptospira.

150
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:14,970
Although they are officially
gram-negative, they don't

151
00:09:14,970 --> 00:09:18,670
stain well and require
special methods such as dark

152
00:09:18,670 --> 00:09:22,550
field or fluorescent
microscopy for visualization.

153
00:09:22,570 --> 00:09:27,500
Examples are shown at
right. They have a corkscrew

154
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:31,650
or spiral morphology.
They have a fagella,

155
00:09:31,650 --> 00:09:35,130
so they are very motile
in liquid phase as well

156
00:09:35,130 --> 00:09:38,790
as in tissue. They are
fastidious and require

157
00:09:38,790 --> 00:09:41,670
very specific growth
requirements in culture.

158
00:09:42,010 --> 00:09:45,990
Transmission is by the
bite of ticks or lice, and

159
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:50,310
rodents and birds act as
reservoirs. Although the

160
00:09:50,310 --> 00:09:54,110
most recent count is about
12 different Borrelia species

161
00:09:54,110 --> 00:09:58,330
that cause Lyme disease,
we will focus on Borrelia

162
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:02,450
burgdorferi since it is
by far the most common.

163
00:10:02,730 --> 00:10:08,090
We will also use Borrelia
miomotoi as an example of

164
00:10:08,090 --> 00:10:11,670
a cause of relapsing fever,
although you can see other

165
00:10:11,670 --> 00:10:14,990
species that cause relapsing
fever on this slide.

166
00:10:16,650 --> 00:10:20,030
Borrelia burgdorferi is
the agent of Lyme disease.

167
00:10:20,150 --> 00:10:24,310
It is geographically
distributed in the Northeast

168
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:27,830
and Central East Coast
and Midwest of the

169
00:10:27,830 --> 00:10:31,710
United States, although
that distribution is

170
00:10:31,710 --> 00:10:36,050
enlarging. And it also
occurs throughout Europe.

171
00:10:36,190 --> 00:10:39,870
There are about half a million
cases every year in the

172
00:10:39,870 --> 00:10:42,990
United States, and that
number has been increasing

173
00:10:42,990 --> 00:10:48,970
every year. It is also
probably vastly underreported.

174
00:10:49,090 --> 00:10:52,470
There are approximately
200,000 cases

175
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:56,530
diagnosed in europe every
year it is transmitted

176
00:10:56,530 --> 00:10:59,930
by the exodes or the
deer tick the incubation

177
00:10:59,930 --> 00:11:03,590
period is three to
thirty days but may be

178
00:11:03,590 --> 00:11:06,630
as short as one to three
days in some persons

179
00:11:07,210 --> 00:11:11,770
stage one is the erythema
migrans rash that

180
00:11:11,770 --> 00:11:15,590
may be associated with
fever and constitutional

181
00:11:15,590 --> 00:11:19,630
symptoms but may not
be in many persons.

182
00:11:19,970 --> 00:11:24,450
Stage two is early
dissemination and stage

183
00:11:24,450 --> 00:11:26,970
three is the late stage
and we will get into

184
00:11:26,970 --> 00:11:30,450
these stages in more
detail on a later slide.

185
00:11:31,170 --> 00:11:34,430
Complications of Lyme
disease include arthritis,

186
00:11:34,690 --> 00:11:35,770
meningitis,

187
00:11:36,130 --> 00:11:39,550
cardiac conduction
abnormalities, as well

188
00:11:39,550 --> 00:11:44,470
as co-infections with
other agents that are

189
00:11:44,680 --> 00:11:48,670
associated with the
same Ixodes deer tick.

190
00:11:49,350 --> 00:11:53,290
The diagnosis is made
clinically in those

191
00:11:53,290 --> 00:11:56,910
in endemic areas who
have erythema migrans.

192
00:11:57,310 --> 00:12:01,690
In those in which the
diagnosis is in question,

193
00:12:01,690 --> 00:12:05,690
there is a two-step
serology method, first

194
00:12:05,690 --> 00:12:10,070
ELISA and then a
confirmatory Western blot.

195
00:12:10,490 --> 00:12:14,370
The organism can
also be visualized

196
00:12:14,370 --> 00:12:16,610
on a wet mount dark field.

197
00:12:16,730 --> 00:12:20,290
The prognosis is
usually excellent with

198
00:12:20,290 --> 00:12:25,190
treatment, but may result
in prolonged sequelae,

199
00:12:25,190 --> 00:12:28,450
such as arthritis or
fatigue in some persons

200
00:12:28,450 --> 00:12:32,310
that is not associated
with active infection.

201
00:12:32,410 --> 00:12:36,690
The treatment is
doxycycline or amoxicillin.

202
00:12:37,270 --> 00:12:39,650
doxycycline is preferred, and

203
00:12:39,650 --> 00:12:41,630
in those with CNS involvement,

204
00:12:41,830 --> 00:12:43,490
ceftriaxone is used.

205
00:12:45,490 --> 00:12:48,710
Borrelia miyamotoi is the cause

206
00:12:48,710 --> 00:12:51,230
of hard tick relapsing fever.

207
00:12:51,470 --> 00:12:54,670
The distribution is
the northeastern, mid

208
00:12:54,670 --> 00:12:57,810
-Atlantic, and upper
Midwest U.S., as well

209
00:12:57,810 --> 00:13:01,150
as California, Europe,
Russia, and Japan.

210
00:13:01,250 --> 00:13:05,150
The disease is much less
common than Lyme disease,

211
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:08,610
although about one to
three percent of those

212
00:13:08,610 --> 00:13:12,810
in endemic areas show
serologic infection.

213
00:13:13,390 --> 00:13:15,430
Transmission is
also by the exodes

214
00:13:15,430 --> 00:13:18,870
tick. The incubation
period is 12 to 16

215
00:13:18,870 --> 00:13:22,010
days, but may be as
long as six weeks.

216
00:13:22,350 --> 00:13:26,950
Clinical features include
fever, which may or may

217
00:13:26,950 --> 00:13:31,630
not last separate, I'm
sorry, several days that is

218
00:13:31,630 --> 00:13:37,070
separated by afebrile
periods. This is the relapse.

219
00:13:37,070 --> 00:13:40,950
The fever is associated
with each relapse with

220
00:13:40,950 --> 00:13:45,150
chills, headache, myologies,
and severe fatigue.

221
00:13:45,570 --> 00:13:48,650
Complications include
meningoencephalitis

222
00:13:48,650 --> 00:13:52,010
and co-infection
with other agents

223
00:13:52,010 --> 00:13:55,990
that are transmitted
by the exodes tick.

224
00:13:56,170 --> 00:14:03,450
Diagnosis is by PCR or
the anti-GLPQ antibody.

225
00:14:03,570 --> 00:14:07,030
Prognosis is excellent
with treatment with

226
00:14:07,030 --> 00:14:10,890
doxycycline, amoxicillin,
or ceftriaxone.

227
00:14:12,890 --> 00:14:16,050
This slide shows the
geographic distribution

228
00:14:16,050 --> 00:14:20,630
of Lyme disease
on the left in the

229
00:14:20,630 --> 00:14:23,090
United States and
the right in Europe.

230
00:14:23,290 --> 00:14:28,710
The darker colors on the
United States map show

231
00:14:28,730 --> 00:14:32,550
larger numbers of infection
and you can see how

232
00:14:32,550 --> 00:14:35,950
it is geographically
distributed to the northeast

233
00:14:35,950 --> 00:14:40,750
and the midwest, mostly
Wisconsin and Michigan.

234
00:14:41,390 --> 00:14:46,070
You have to know though
that this area is

235
00:14:46,070 --> 00:14:50,030
expanding every year
both to the south and

236
00:14:50,030 --> 00:14:52,390
to the west, and there
are more and more

237
00:14:52,390 --> 00:14:57,010
cases diagnosed in
those areas every year.

238
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:03,510
And the stages of
Lyme disease a little

239
00:15:03,510 --> 00:15:06,110
earlier, but let's go
